    "58da5270-5392-4698-9a87-b6b21fa071e3": {
      "filters": null,
      "name": "The two lakes, before and after",
      "parameters": {
        "bearing": null,
        "content": {
          "attachments": {},
          "contentMode": "markdown",
          "image": "",
          "imageCaption": "",
          "markdown": "**The same two boxes, before the flood and after it.**\n\n![The two barrier lakes, before and after](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/arjxn-py/nepal-flood-26-aug/main/data/nepal-flood-2026-review/fig-barrier-lakes.png)\n\nThe upper lake, 20 ha, sits 160 m below the edge\nof the scar. It is held in by what came off the mountain. The lower lake,\n12 ha, is 4.6 km north-west. Its valley has gone\nfrom forest to pale sand.\n\nIn normal colour, pale ground and water look alike. In near infrared they do not,\nbecause water absorbs it. Inside the upper outline, the water index goes from\n-0.01 before the flood to +0.05 after. The share of pixels above zero goes from\n39 to 99 per cent. Water would move it in that direction, across about that much\nof the outline. But it would usually move it further.\n\nThe outlines are UNOSAT's, from CARTOSAT-3 of 28 August.\n",
          "paneAlignment": "start",
          "panelWidth": "34%"
        },
        "enableIdentify": false,
        "extent": [
          9517259.865370926,
          3288367.1032997635,
          9517259.865370926,
          3288367.1032997635
        ],
