    "c4d16fba-ea2a-4ffe-a1ba-3fade7917b73": {
      "filters": null,
      "name": "Why the three are different",
      "parameters": {
        "bearing": null,
        "content": {
          "attachments": {},
          "contentMode": "markdown",
          "image": "",
          "imageCaption": "",
          "markdown": "**One thing explains all three: how steeply the riverbed falls.**\n\n![Bed gradient and bridge losses over the same three reaches](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/arjxn-py/nepal-flood-26-aug/main/data/nepal-flood-2026-review/fig-gradient-vs-damage.png)\n\nA steep bed makes fast water. Fast water hits the bank with everything it is\ncarrying. Where the bed is flat, the river spreads out instead of cutting in.\n\n**16 of 16** bridges were lost in the\ngorge. **6 of 108** on the plain. In its report of\n1 September, NDRRMA counted **41 road bridges washed\naway** and 4 damaged along the whole river.\n",
          "paneAlignment": "start",
          "panelWidth": "58%"
        },
        "enableIdentify": false,
        "extent": [
          9447117.454222083,
          3249490.7156830165,
          9447117.454222083,
          3249490.7156830165
        ],
        "layerOverride": [
          {
